---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Privacy policy — Milton"
  description="Milton is operated by Zone of Danger, LLC (“we”)."
  legal
>
  <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
  <p class="updated">
    Last updated: <time datetime="2026-11-06">November 6, 2026</time>
  </p>
  <p>
    Milton is operated by Zone of Danger, LLC (“we”). Milton keeps the writing
    you approved, word for word. It never rewrites a rule or a sample, and it
    never reads your inbox. The details follow.
  </p>

  <h2>What Milton stores</h2>
  <p style="text-wrap:pretty">
    Your name and email address from Google or GitHub when you sign in; the
    writing rules you ask your assistants to save; the samples of your writing
    you approve for saving, stored exactly as approved, including any names they
    contain; each rule and sample you saved, with its versions and which
    assistant saved it; request receipts used to prevent duplicate saves;
    internal records of accepted changes, including the exact submitted rules,
    edits or samples, authorship and affected versions; connected assistants and
    the assistants you disconnect; counts of tool calls you make each day; and
    your billing record: your Stripe customer and subscription IDs, the
    subscription’s status, its trial and billing period dates, and which trial
    reminders have been sent.
  </p>

  <h2>How we use it</h2>
  <p>
    Only to run Milton: to sign you in, store your rules, retain their internal
    history, prepare the cover sheet and reports for the assistants you connect,
    and run your trial and subscription. Accepted-change records remain with
    your internal history until account deletion. Your data is encrypted at rest
    by our database provider. We do not sell your data, show ads, or use your
    rules or samples to train AI models.
  </p>

  <h2>Who can see your rules and samples</h2>
  <p>
    You and the assistants you connect receive your current cover sheet and
    reports, samples included. Once an assistant has read them, its handling of
    that content is governed by that assistant’s own terms and privacy policy,
    not ours. Rules and samples are stored exactly as your assistant sends them;
    Ordinary saves and reads do not send them to an AI model. If you explicitly
    opt into the private guide pilot, the background processing described below
    sends approved samples to Anthropic. Milton does not collect your assistant
    conversations. The service providers below process data to operate Milton.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can disconnect an assistant at any time from Connected assistants in the
    Milton app. Milton blocks further access, even with an existing
    authorization. This does not erase content the assistant already received.
    To use it again, reconnect it and approve access.
  </p>

  <h2>Samples</h2>
  <p style="text-wrap:pretty">
    A sample is saved only after you say yes to a specific piece of writing, and
    it is stored exactly as you approved it. Milton does not remove names or
    other details from it; you decide what to save. Saving a new sample keeps
    your existing samples unless you ask to replace or remove them. You can ask
    your assistant to replace or remove a sample. Removed or replaced samples
    leave future reports and current downloads, but remain in your internal
    history until account deletion. This does not erase copies an assistant
    already received.
  </p>

  <h2>Optional derived guides</h2>
  <p>
    Guides are off by default. For eligible accounts that explicitly opt in,
    Milton sends every active approved sample in a report, with its approved
    classification and context, to Anthropic to infer writing tendencies. Rules,
    retired sample revisions and earlier guides are excluded. The resulting
    guide is an interpretation, shown to you and the assistants you connect;
    your approved writing stays unchanged. A stale guide can still reflect a
    sample that was corrected or removed until its update succeeds. Removing all
    active samples clears the guide immediately. Guides are excluded from your
    download.
  </p>
  <p>
    Anthropic’s standard API policy deletes inputs and outputs within 30 days,
    with exceptions for policy enforcement, legal requirements and contractual
    terms. This pilot does not promise zero data retention. See
    <a href="https://privacy.claude.com/en/articles/7996866-how-long-do-you-store-my-organization-s-data">
      Anthropic’s retention policy
    </a>
    . Disabling participation hides guides on future reads; it cannot retract
    copies already received by an assistant. Completed guides may remain stored
    while disabled, until account deletion. Milton keeps one current guide per
    report, without a guide history archive.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it is kept</h2>
  <p>
    The database and sign-in run on Neon (United States). The public website
    runs on Vercel; the app and MCP server run on Google Cloud Run (United
    States). The public website uses Vercel Web Analytics, which sets no
    cookies: it records each page view’s address and query parameters, referrer,
    time, browser, operating system, device type and location down to city, and
    counts visitors with a daily-reset hash of the request instead of tying
    records to a person or IP address. DNS is on Cloudflare. Google and GitHub
    also handle your sign-in with them. Stripe processes subscriptions and
    payments.
  </p>

  <h2>Billing</h2>
  <p>
    Milton creates a Stripe customer for you when your free trial starts, and
    starts your trial subscription at Stripe with no card. If you had no trial,
    the customer is created when you first open billing. Milton gives Stripe
    your Milton account ID and, if Milton has it at that moment, your sign-in
    email address. Each time you open billing from the Milton app, Milton sends
    Stripe your current sign-in email address for that customer.
  </p>
  <p>
    You enter card and other payment details on Stripe’s pages, not Milton’s.
    They go to Stripe, and Milton does not store them.
  </p>

  <h2>Cookies</h2>
  <p>Only the ones that keep you signed in.</p>

  <h2>Deleting your account</h2>
  <p>
    Choose Delete account in the Milton app. Deletion removes your rules,
    samples, derived guides and pending guide work, internal memos and their
    history, your cover sheet and reports, request receipts, accepted-change
    records, usage counts, and Milton’s link to your sign-in, and your billing
    record. It also disconnects every assistant you connected and signs you out.
    If you have a Stripe customer, deletion deletes it at Stripe, which ends any
    subscription immediately, with no refund.
  </p>
  <p>These records remain after deletion:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A record of each assistant you connected (your sign-in ID and the
      assistant’s ID, with no content), so its earlier access cannot reach a new
      account.
    </li>
    <li>
      A one-way fingerprint of your sign-in, so a new account does not receive a
      second free trial.
    </li>
    <li>
      Your sign-in record (name, email address and Google or GitHub link), which
      stays with Neon, our sign-in provider. Email
      <a href="mailto:support@meetmilton.com">support@meetmilton.com</a> to have
      it removed.
    </li>
    <li>
      Content-free guide spending records (amount, time and a one-way account
      fingerprint), so deletion cannot reset pilot spending limits.
    </li>
    <li>Stripe keeps past invoices and payment records.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Copies in database backups are not erased immediately; they are removed when
    those backups expire or are deleted. You can also email
    <a href="mailto:support@meetmilton.com">support@meetmilton.com</a> and we
    will delete your account for you.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>We will update the date above when this policy changes.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="mailto:support@meetmilton.com">support@meetmilton.com</a>.
  </p>
</SiteLayout>
